"Near-Field 3D Localization via MIMO Radar: Cram&#x00E9;r-Rao Bound Analysis and Estimator Design (https://www.weizmann.ac.il/math/yonina/sites/math.yonina/files/publications/Near-Field_3D_Localization_via_MIMO_Radar_Cramr-Rao_Bound_Analysis_and_Estimator_Design.pdf)\nciteturn217search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 2.1 years ago; This paper studied a near-field MIMO radar system for multi-target localization by considering the exact spherical wavefront model with both channel phase and amplitude variations across antennas. ... According to the Slepian-Bangs formula [44, B.3.25],\nAccording to the Slepian-Bangs formula [44, B.3.25],\n\n\\[\n\\tilde{\\mathbf{F}}(\\tilde{\\theta}_i,\\tilde{\\theta}_j)\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left[\nC^{-1}(\\tilde{\\theta}) \\frac{\\partial C(\\tilde{\\theta})}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}\nC^{-1}(\\tilde{\\theta}) \\frac{\\partial C(\\tilde{\\theta})}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j}\n\\right]\n+\n2\\Re\\!\\left[\n\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\mu}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}\\right)^{H}\nC^{-1}(\\tilde{\\theta})\n\\frac{\\partial \\mu}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j}\n\\right].\n\\tag{70}\n\\]\n\nAs \\(C(\\tilde{\\theta})\\) is a block diagonal matrix, the inverse of \\(C(\\tilde{\\theta})\\) can be obtained via the inverse of each individual block matrix \\(Q\\) in \\(C(\\tilde{\\theta})\\). Thus, the first term in (70) is rewritten as\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}\\left\\{\n\\begin{bmatrix}\nQ^{-1}\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}Q^{-1}\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j} & 0 & 0 \\\\\n0 & \\ddots & 0 \\\\\n0 & 0 & Q^{-1}\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}Q^{-1}\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j}\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\right\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich corresponds to the first term \\(L\\,\\mathrm{tr}\\!\\left[Q^{-1}\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}Q^{-1}\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j}\\right]\\) in (14). The second term in (70) is rewritten as\n\n\\[\n2\\Re\\left\\{\n\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\n\\left(\n\\frac{\\partial(ABV^T x_l)}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}\n\\right)^{H}\nQ^{-1}\n\\left(\n\\frac{\\partial(ABV^T x_l)}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j}\n\\right)\n\\right\\}\n\\tag{71}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n=\n2\\Re\\,\\operatorname{tr}\\left[\n\\left(\\frac{\\partial(ABV^T X)}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_i}\\right)^{H}\nQ^{-1}\n\\left(\\frac{\\partial(ABV^T X)}{\\partial \\tilde{\\theta}_j}\\right)\n\\right],\n\\tag{72}\n\\]\n\nwhich is the second term in (14), completing the proof.\n\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: Weizmann Institute of Science. Downloaded on September 12,2024 at 12:20:27 UTC from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImportance sampling based direct maximum likelihood position determination of multiple emitters using finite measurements - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165168421001493)\nciteturn217search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; We resort to Pincus’ theorem and the IS technique to approximate the exact ML solution of the problem of direct position determination of multiple emitters with finite measurements. ... In this paper, we have researched the direct ML position determination of dense emitters using finite observation resources.\n\n## Signal Processing\n\nDate: September 2021\n\nArticle: 108111\n\nVolume: Volume 186\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n\n# Importance sampling based direct maximum likelihood position determination of multiple emitters using finite measurements\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Kegang Hao, Qun Wan\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.sigpro.2021.108111\n\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * •\n\nWe resort to Pincus’ theorem and the IS technique to approximate the exact ML solution of the problem of direct position determination of multiple emitters with finite measurements.\n\n  * •\n\nWe design a factorable importance function to generate the required parameter realizations efficiently.\n\n  * •\n\nCompared with the exponential complexity of the exhaustive multidimensional ML searching, the complexity of the new approach is moderate.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe exact Direct Maximum Likelihood Position Determination (ML-DPD) of multiple emitters requires a multidimensional searching with exponential complexity. The subspace-based DPD technique and the filtering-based DPD technique are recently proposed to reduce the complexity by transforming the original high-dimensional optimization problem into several low-dimensional ones. However, these techniques approximate the exact ML solution only if the measurements are assumed infinite. In this paper, we model the received signals in the nonlinear regression form and resort to the multidimensional optimization framework, consisting of the Pincus’ theorem and the Importance Sampling (IS) technique, to approximate the exact ML solution using finite measurements. The proposed IS based ML DPD approach also reduces the complexity by decoupling the multidimensional optimization problem into several three-dimensional ones. Moreover, the new non-iterative ML DPD approach guarantees global optimality and does not suffer from the off-grid problems inherent to most DPD techniques. The numerical simulations show that the proposed ML DPD estimator acquires the exact multidimensional ML solution even using a narrow bandwidth or being at low Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR).\n\n## Keywords\n\nDirect position determination\n\n;\n\nMean maximum likelihood\n\n;\n\nImportance sampling\n\n;\n\nMultiple emitters\n\n;\n\nFinite measurements\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe source localization technique has been studied for several decades and applied in many practical scenarios, such as the Location-Based Services (LBS) in communication systems, objects tracking in Internet of Things (IoT) and targets detection in radar system. The classical localization methods are two-step processing. Firstly, intermediate parameters that rely on the locations of the sources, like the Angle of Arrival (AOA) and the Time of Arrival (TOA), are estimated from the measurements of different receivers respectively. Then, the locations of sources are estimated based on these intermediate parameters. The position methods based on different intermediate parameters have been proposed. The array processing methods for position by AOA are reviewed by Krim and Viberg comprehensively [1]. The classical closed-form Least Squares (LS) solution using TDOA was derived by Smith and Abel [2]. To be specific, the MUSIC-DPD [12] and MVDR-DPD [14], [15] both need lots of observation samples to approximate the covariance matrix by the sample covariance matrix. The MF-DPD [13] also need sufficient observation samples to decouple the problem based on the independence among transmitted signals. Unfortunately, the length of observation window is usually limited for the real-time position request or the stationary assumption. Thus, the number of observation samples is finite and the above estimators can not achieve the ML performance in practice. Besides, the iterative ML method [16] is proposed to achieve the exact ML solution efficiently but its convergence to the global optimum relies on the initial estimate.\n\nIn order to achieve the exact ML solution with finite observation resources, in this paper we resort to the optimization framework consisting of the Pincus’ theorem [17] and the IS technique [18]. We design a factorable probability density function (pdf) on model parameters. This pdf allows a very easy sampling in the vicinity of the exact ML solution. In fact, since {\\mathit{w}}_{j}\\left(l\\right) in (7) are independent among different sections of different received signals and follow the identity distribution {\\mathit{w}}_{j}\\left(l\\right)\\sim N(\\mathit{0},{\\sigma }^{2}{\\mathit{I}}_{NM}), it can be shown that the Log Likelihood Function (LLF), of which the constant terms are dropped, is given by,\\mathcal{L}(\\mathit{P},\\stackrel{˚}{\\mathit{t}},\\mathit{\\alpha })\\triangleq -\\sum _{j=1}^{{N}_{r}}\\sum _{l=1}^{L}{\\parallel {\\mathit{y}}_{j}\\left(l\\right)-{\\mathit{\\Gamma }}_{j,l}(\\mathit{P},\\stackrel{˚}{\\mathit{t}}){\\mathit{\\alpha }}_{j}\\parallel }^{2}Where \\parallel ·\\parallel denotes the vector 2-norm. To\n\n### Global maximization of the CLF\n\nLike the previous works within other estimation problems [18], [21], [22], [23], [24], we resort to the optimization framework [18], consisting of the Pincus’ theorem [17] and the IS technique, to efficiently approximate the global solution of the multidimensional ML problem, using finite observation resources such as the observation time, the signal bandwidth and the array aperture. According to the definition of {g}_{\\mathit{p}}^{\\text{mar}}({x}_{q},{y}_{q}) (A.8), its\n\n### Numerical simulation and discussion\n\nIn this section, we design several numerical simulations to evaluate the performance of the proposed IS based ML DPD estimator, denoted by IS-ML-DPD, and compare it with the decoupled ML DPD estimator proposed in Weiss and Amar [13] and the MVDR based DPD estimator proposed in Tirer and Weiss [14], denoted by single-ML-DPD and MVDR-DPD respectively. To give an explicit comparison with the exact ML estimator (14), we also give its performance curve that are obtained by an iterative local\n\n### Conclusion\n\nIn this paper, we have researched the direct ML position determination of dense emitters using finite observation resources. We resort to a multidimensional optimization framework, consisting of the Pincus’ theorem and the IS technique, to transform the multidimensional ML problem into several single ones. We factorized the target pdf to design the appropriate IFs that are used to generate the required realizations. The numerical simulations show that the proposed IS based ML DPD approach\n\n### CRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nKegang Hao: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Writing - original draft, Writing - review & editing. Qun Wan: Validation, Resources, Supervision.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResearch On Optimizing Parameter Grid For Direct Position Determination Of Emitters (https://globethesis.com/?t=1488306524973549)\nciteturn217search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Research On Optimizing Parameter Grid For Direct Position Determination Of Emitters ... 3)To ruduce the computational complexity of the exhaustive searching,based on the Pincus theory the multi--target DPD problem is transformed into multiple single--target position expectation problems.According to the importance sampling technique,the exhaustive searching is replaced with parameter sampling and an importance sampling based maximum likelihood DPD algorithm is proposed.As a result,the desired position expectations are estimated using the parameter realizations and the inherent grid quantization error is eliminated.Compared with the exsisting DPD algorithms,the proposed algorithm has better performance with finite observation resources.(\n\n# Research On Optimizing Parameter Grid For Direct Position Determination Of Emitters\n\nPosted on:2022-06-06 Degree:Doctor Type:Dissertation\nCountry:China Candidate:K G Hao Full Text:PDF\nGTID:1488306524973549 Subject:Signal and Information Processing\nAbstract/Summary:\nThe performance of traditional two-step positioning approach degrades when it is applied in complex scenarios(such as indoor scenario and scenario with finite observation resources).In order to improve the sub-optimal positioning performance,researchers propose a novel and optimal positioning approach-the Direct Position Determination(DPD)approach.The DPD approach jointly processes all measurments and directly es-timates the target position from the raw measurments,which avoids the information loss caused by the step-by-step processing in two-step positioning approach.Consequently,the DPD approach has drawn much attention and can significantly promote the position-ing performance in complex applications.As a new and developing approach,the DPD has its own problems to be solved.For example,the multi--target DPD problem is a big challenge because of the curse of dimensionality and the high computational complexity of exhaustive searching is intractable.Besides,meshing the parameter space results in the model quantization error inherently.Regarding the problems mentioned above,in this dissertation,the DPD approaches for emitters in different scenarios and its application to the indoor communication system are studied from the view of optimizing the parameter grid.The main contributions of the dissertation are summarized as follows:(1)With the purpose of jointly estimating the order and parameters of the DPD model and eliminating the quantization error,the indoor DPD model is built using the dynamic position grid and under the framework of sparse recovery a sparse Bayesian learning based indoor DPD algorithm is proposed,by combining the EM algorithm and the GD algorithm.Compared with the exsisting fixed grid based DPD algorithms,the proposed algorithm is more robust to the multipath interference and has better positioning performance.(2)In order to jointly solve the multi-target DPD problem and the ”intermittent emis-sion-sources” association problem,the emitter positions are regarded as the associa-tion labels and the emission statuses are estimated using the K-means clustering algorithm.A MVDR based DPD algorithm for multiple intermittent emitters is proposed,by decou-pling the emission statuses and the emitter positions.By comparison with the exsisting alternative projection based DPD algorithm,we show that the proposed algorithm does not rely on the prior information about the number of emitters and the initial position estimations.(3)To ruduce the computational complexity of the exhaustive searching,based on the Pincus theory the multi--target DPD problem is transformed into multiple single--target position expectation problems.According to the importance sampling technique,the exhaustive searching is replaced with parameter sampling and an importance sampling based maximum likelihood DPD algorithm is proposed.As a result,the desired position expectations are estimated using the parameter realizations and the inherent grid quantization error is eliminated.Compared with the exsisting DPD algorithms,the proposed algorithm has better performance with finite observation resources.(4)A FFT based TDOA DPD algorithm and its application to the indoor commu-nication system are studied.The computational load of the TDOA position spectrum is decreased by two order of magnitudes.Compared to the two-step positioning approach,the proposed algorithm has better performance with cost of increasing few computational load.The mentioned DPD algorithms and implementation scheme are verified to be ef-fective using numerical simulations or real data collected from the indoor communication system.\nKeywords/Search Tags: Direct Position Determination, Indoor Positioning, Multi-target Positioning, Sparse Bayesian Learning, Importance Sampling--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Choice of Basis Functions for Modeling Earth&apos;s Elastic Deformation Due To Surface Loading - Yang - 2025 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth - Wiley Online Library (https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1029/2025JB031662)\nciteturn217search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; The Slepian functions (also known as Slepian bases or concentration functions) are designed to optimize spatial localization while maintaining spectral resolution.\n## 8 Slepian Function\n\n### 8.1 Theory\n\nThe Slepian functions (also known as Slepian bases or concentration functions) are designed to optimize spatial localization while maintaining spectral resolution. Specifically, they maximize the concentration of energy in both the spatial and spectral domains (Albertella et al., 1999; Simons et al., 2006). A sequence of orthogonal Slepian functions can be used to represent surface mass/density or vertical displacement, yielding:\n\n$$ ${G}_{U}(\\theta ,\\lambda )=a\\sum\\limits _{i}^{{(N+1)}^{2}}{S}_{i}{g}_{i}(\\theta ,\\lambda ),$$$ (26)\n\nwhere $ ${g}_{i}$$ represents the $ $i\\mathrm{t}\\mathrm{h}$$ Slepian function and $ ${S}_{i}$$ is its coefficient, $ $N$$ indicates the band-limited spectrum of each Slepian function, in terms of spherical harmonics, following\n\n$$ ${g}_{i}(\\theta ,\\lambda )=\\sum\\limits _{n=0}^{N}\\sum\\limits _{m=-n}^{m=n}{g}_{i,nm}{\\bar{Y}}_{nm}(\\lambda ,\\theta ),$$$ (27)\n\nwhere one can see that each Slepian function is defined on the entire globe, that is, $ ${\\Omega }$$. However, since the Slepian function is designed for optimal localization, its energy is expected to be mainly concentrated in a given region $ $\\mathbf{R}$$. The optimal function is obtained when the ratio between the power of $ ${g}_{i}(\\theta ,\\lambda )$$ within the target $ $\\mathbf{R}$$ and within the globe $ ${\\Omega }$$ is maximized:\n\n$$ ${\\beta }_{i}={\\int }_{\\mathbf{R}}{g}_{i}^{2}(\\theta ,\\lambda )d\\omega /{\\int }_{{\\Omega }}{g}_{i}^{2}(\\theta ,\\lambda )d\\omega $$$ (28)\n\nwhere $ ${\\beta }_{i}$$ (0$ ${\\le} {\\beta }_{i}\\le $$1) indicates the degree of energy concentration of each basis function. Be aware that the eigenfunctions (eigenvector $ ${g}_{i}(\\theta ,\\lambda )$$, eigenvalue $ ${\\beta }_{i}$$) of a Fredholm integral equation provide the solution to this maximization problem. The readers can refer to Albertella et al. (1999), Han and Razeghi (2017), and Shen et al. (2024) for more details on their derivation and implementation.\n\nSuppose a band-limited spectrum up to $ $N=60$$, the number of corresponding Slepian functions as depicted in Equation 26 should be 3,721. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptimal RIS Placement in Near-Field Localization Using Gray (https://d197for5662m48.cloudfront.net/documents/publicationstatus/239135/preprint_pdf/22370a23942317e7435194f41bb6775b.pdf)\nciteturn217search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; localization problem. ... given by the Slepian-Bangs formula [13, Section 3.9] as\nand the localization parameters and obtain the error bounds for\n3D localization.\nA. FIM of Channel Parameters\nWe consider the vector of channel parameters as\nηch ≜[rT, τ◦, τr, ρT\n◦, ρT]T ∈R(N+6).\n(5)\nThe UE’s position, denoted by pu ∈R3, pu = [xu, yu, zu]T,\nremains unknown. We denote the known position of the BS,\nand each element of the RIS as pb ∈R3, pb = [xb, yb, zb]T,\nand pr,i ∈R3, pr,i = [xr,i, yr,i, zr,i]T, respectively. The center of\nthe RIS’s position is shown by pr ∈R3, pr = [xr, yr, zr]T.\nThe RIS is oriented parallel to the Y-Z plane and comprises\nN elements. The parameters dbu, dru, and dbr represent the\ndistances between BS-UE, RIS-UE, and BS-RIS, respectively.\nThese distances can be expressed as\nwhere r = [r1, . . . , rN]T, ρ◦= [ℜ{ρ◦}, ℑ{ρ◦}]T, and ρ =\n[ℜ{ρ}, ℑ{ρ}]T\n(xb −xr)2 + (yb −yr)2 + (zb −zr)2,\n(1)\ndbr = ∥pb −pr∥=\nq\nThe FIM of ηch, by considering the signal model (4), is\ngiven by the Slepian-Bangs formula [13, Section 3.9] as\n(xr −xu)2 + (yr −yu)2 + (zr −zu)2,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SIGNAL PROCESSING, VOL. 58, NO. 5, MAY 2010 (https://alexandrerenaux.github.io/publications/%5BJ9%5D.pdf)\nciteturn217search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Near-Field Source Localization ... of this bound using the extended Slepian–Bangs formula for a uniform,\nshman et al. [15] provided a direct (but similar) matrix-based derivation\nof this bound using the extended Slepian–Bangs formula for a uniform,\na nonuniform, and an unknown noise ﬁeld, respectively. On the other\nhand, a matrix-based expression of the CCRB for the far-ﬁeld case was\nderived by Stoica et al. in [16].\nUnlike the far-ﬁeld case, the CRB for the near-ﬁeld localization\nproblem has been less studied. One can ﬁnd in [17] matrix-based\nexpressions of the UCRB for range and bearing estimation. Ottersten\net al. derived a general matrix-based expressions of the UCRB for\nunknown parameters associated with the emitted signal [10]. Recently,\nGrosicki et al. [6] extended, to the near-ﬁeld case, the matrix-form\nexpression for the UCRB similar to that given in [12] in the far-ﬁeld\ncase. Again, one should note that all the closed-form expressions,\ngiven in the literature and above concerning the near-ﬁeld case, are\nmatrix-based expressions stopped before the inversion of the Fisher\ninformation matrix. To the best of our knowledge, no non-matrix\nexpressions are available concerning the CCRB and UCRB for range\nand bearing estimation in the near-ﬁeld context. The goal of this\ncorrespondence is to ﬁll this lack. Particularly, non-matrix closed-form\nexpressions of the CRB in the case of a single deterministic (but\nunknown) and stochastic time-varying narrowband source in the\nnear-ﬁeld region are derived and analyzed. Consequently, this ap-\nproach avoids the costly computational cost of the matrix-based\nCRB expressions particularly for a large number of snapshots (for the\nCCRB) and/or for a large number of sensors (for the UCRB). However,\nit is not the only reason concerning the usefulness of these non-matrix\nexpressions. Deriving non-matrix expressions of the CRB enables us\n1053-587X/$26.00 © 2010 IEEE\nAuthorized licensed use limited to: Pascal Larzabal. Downloaded on May 04,2010 at 14:09:40 UTC from IEEE Xplore.  Restrictions apply.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhysical Review Applied - Browse by Subject (https://journals.aps.org/prapplied/subjects?journal%5B%5D=prapplied&page=22&sort=recent&subject_area%5B%5D=optics&subject_area%5B%5D=plasmonics&type=article)\nciteturn217search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; ## Slepian eigenvalues for near-field communication\n## Erratum: Entanglement source and quantum memory analysis for zero-added-loss multiplexing [Phys. Rev. Applied 22, 044014 (2024)]\n\nJeffrey H. Shapiro, Michael G. Raymer, Clark Embleton, Franco N. C. Wong, and Brian J. Smith\n\nPhys. Rev. Applied 23, 029901 (2025) - Published 27 February, 2025\n\n## Slepian eigenvalues for near-field communication\n\nStephen C. Creagh and Gabriele Gradoni\n\nPhys. Rev. Applied 23, 024070 (2025) - Published 27 February, 2025\n\nShow Abstract\n\nWe generalize an approximation of eigenvalues associated with projection onto a rectangular patch of phase space so that it applies to patches with curved boundaries. This more general case is relevant to characterizing degrees of freedom and communication rates in multiple-input–multiple-output arrays and allows previous results derived under far-field assumptions to be applied to near-field scenarios. The underlying approximation is found by our relating these eigenvalues to tunneling probabilities in an associated quantum-mechanical Hamiltonian, which is generalized here to cope with deformed geometries by a semiclassical transformation of the problem to the traditionally studied rectangular one.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1\nFundamental Limits for Near-Field Sensing — Part (https://www.znakschoola.ru/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2512.24958%23UE7DkG0PaBHZOakR54g1Wl8%3D)\nciteturn217search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Slepian–Bangs formulation, we derive closed-form Cram´er–Rao bounds ... Complementary work has investigated near-ﬁeld localization\n1\nFundamental Limits for Near-Field Sensing — Part I:\nNarrow-Band Systems\nTong Wei, Member, IEEE, Kumar Vijay Mishra, Senior Member, IEEE, Bhavani Shankar M.R., Senior Member, IEEE, Bj¨orn\nOttersten, Fellow, IEEE\nAbstract—Extremely large-scale antenna arrays (ELAAs) envisioned\nfor 6G enable high-resolution sensing. However, the ELAAs worked\nin extremely high frequency will push operation into the near-ﬁeld\nregion, where spherical wavefronts invalidate classical far-ﬁeld models\nand alter fundamental estimation limits. The purpose of this and the\ncompanion paper (Part II) is to develop the theory of fundamental\nlimits for near-ﬁeld sensing systems in detail. In this paper (Part I),\nwe develop a uniﬁed narrow-band near-ﬁeld signal model for joint\nparameter sensing of moving targets using the ELAAs. Leveraging the\nSlepian–Bangs formulation, we derive closed-form Cram´er–Rao bounds\n(CRBs) for joint estimation of target position, velocity, and radar\ncross-section (RCS) under the slow-time sampling model. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDISTRIBUTED CANCELLATION-BASED MULTIPLE-SOURCE LOCALIZATION (https://dimitris-ampeliotis.github.io/pdfs/C3.pdf)\nciteturn217search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; In this work, a multiple-source localization algorithm for sensor net- ... source localization and unknown sensor location estimation forwideband signals in the near field”, IEEE Transactions on Sig- ... the central 50×50 area to avoid boundary effects.\nwideband signals in the near field”, IEEE Transactions on Sig-\nnal Processing, Vol. 50, No. 8, August 2002\n[10] H. Garcia-Molina. ”Elections in a distributed computing sys-\ntem”, IEEE Transactions on Computers, C-31(1):47–59, Jan-\nuary 1982.\n[11] R. Motwani and P. Ragavan. Randomized Algorithms. Cam-\nbridge University Press, 1995.\nIn this experiment, 50 sensors were placed randomly (uniformly)\nover an area of 100 × 100 meters and two unequal power sources\n(A1 = 10000,A2 = 5000) were randomly (uniformly) placed over\nthe central 50×50 area to avoid boundary effects. Here, the source\nwith power A1 is conside